WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.500
Bismillah wa alhamdulillah wa salatu wa
salam ala rasulullah. Hello calculus

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:06.860
students. Today we're going to be taking
derivatives with the number e. Let's get

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:11.870
into it. As we all know, when you have y
equals e to the x, the derivative

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.650
is itself. And we're going to use this
property to answer all of our examples.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.910
But one thing about what this property means is
that almost every single time you're

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.070
taking a derivative of e to some variable,
you're going to have to use the

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.490
chain rule, because here, for example,
when y equals [e to the 8x],

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.530
this just falls under f of g of x, where

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.910
f of x is equal to [e to the x] and g of x is

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:48.330
equal to [8x], right, because it's always going to
be in that composite form. So what

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.030
you're always going to have to do is end up
taking the power of e and then the power

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.880
of what's inside. It's pretty easy to
visualize with Leibniz notation.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:04.170
So if instead we just went
here, y prime equals

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.740
d e to the 8x over [d of]

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:10.040
8x

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:16.840
times d of 8x over d of x, you can really

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:20.800
easily see that all that's happening is because
here we're deriving with respect to

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.720
what's inside, it's going to become e to the 8x
again. And we're going to multiply 8

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.860
because 8 times x, when you take the power rule
of x, it's just going to be 8. So it

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:35.160
just equals 8 e to the 8x. Super
easy. Here, same thing.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:41.640
If we want, we can do it this time
just with the f of g

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.400
of x

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.470
equals f prime of g of x times g prime of

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.810
x. So here, what that would be is you would have
e [to the] x squared. We're just going to

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:57.910
copy that down. We're going to multiply it by
the derivative of what's inside. The

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.770
derivative of x squared is just 2x, so you're
going to be left with 2x e to the [x squared].

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.690
Here, let's use [Leibniz] again.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.690
We would do [y prime] equals d of e
to the 3x squared

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:18.130
plus 7x, all over d [of] 3x
squared plus 7x.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:23.390
And I'm multiplying this by d of the 3x
squared plus 7x all over

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:23.910
dx.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:30.270
Again, this just gives us the exact same
number, e to the 3x squared plus

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.630
7x times 6x plus 7.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.990
If you want, you can put this in front, but
it's the same answer. It won't do

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.460
anything. On to our next example here, we have
y equals x squared times e to the

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:47.460
negative x. Here, the only thing we're going
to add on is now we have to do the

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:52.960
product rule as well, because both of these
terms have x's in them. And doing

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:58.260
our product rule, we know it is f
prime times g of x [plus] f

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:03.580
times g prime of x. So if we want to use Leibniz
notation, we can have here d of x

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:08.460
squared over dx times e to the
negative x plus

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:13.740
x squared times d of e to the negative x

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:19.220
over d of the negative x times d
of the negative x

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.520
over dx.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:30.600
This will be, we're applying the power
rule to our d [of] x squared over

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.960
dx, that gives us 2x, and then we are going to
multiply that [by] e to the negative x, and

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:41.440
then we will have plus x squared, and we will
have, [when] we do the derivative of e to

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:46.480
the negative x over d [of] negative x, it's
just e to the negative x times

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:51.870
the derivative of negative x, which is
a minus 1. This gives

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:57.240
us 2x e to the negative x minus x squared e
to the negative x. We're factoring out

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:01.600
x e to the negative x here, and x e to the
negative x here, and that leaves us with

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:02.660
2 minus x.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:09.910
Now on to our final example. So here we want
to find the second derivative of

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.030
y equals x times e to the 2x. We start by
taking our first derivative, and once

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.310
again, we're going to apply that [product] rule,
but this time let's not use Leibniz

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.470
notation. The [product] rule tells us we need
f prime times g of x plus f

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.910
times g prime of x. f prime here is 1, so we're
going to do 1 times g of x, which

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.110
is e to the 2x, and we are going to
add f times g prime of x.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.980
f is x. g prime is e to the 2x times 2.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:44.100
Expanding this further, we get e to
the 2x plus [2x] e to

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:44.740
the 2x.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.970
Now we take the second derivative. Second
derivative, we'll be taking the derivatives

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:56.290
on their own. The derivative of the first
term is e to the 2x times 2,

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:00.770
and then if we take the derivative [of] our second
term, we can actually do a trick here,

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:05.750
because since this derivative is
the same as if I put 2

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:11.210
times [x e to the 2x]. x e to the

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.450
2x is actually what we took when we had our
first derivative. So [the derivative of] x e to the 2x

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.890
equals all of this together. So what I can do is
I can keep that 2 out and then put

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:25.370
in everything we got for the derivative of
x e to the 2x, which is e to

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.770
the 2x plus 2x e to the 2x.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.830
Expanding that further, we get 2 e to
the 2x plus 2 e to the 2x

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.250
plus 4 x e to the 2x. This gives us

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:45.650
4 e to the 2x plus 4x e to the 2x.
If we want to factor

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.390
this, this will be 4 e to the 2x, 1 plus x.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.270
And that is our e questions. As you can
see, they're not that bad.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.190
I think, in my opinion, this unit is much easier
than the last unit because of the

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.010
word problems if you guys
struggle with them.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.650
But yeah, we have a couple more concepts in
Unit 3, but they shouldn't be that

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:08.990
difficult.
